A||N||15||gkozlov||Using fiberglass...||||1||
Z||000000||gkozlov||02-21-2001||06:46 AM||||I like many others have decided to copycat Stimpy.<P>Question is how do I build up the fiberglass. The encloser is very thin. How do I go about making it thicker? <P>The fiberglass so far is only paper thin. If somebody could please guide me in the right direction in using and working with fiberglass. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">     <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A><BR>MSN MESSENGER: wkozlov@hotmail.com||||reg||1
Z||000001||mbramble||02-21-2001||09:16 AM||||To make it thicker, you simply add layers of fiberglass matting/cloth and resin until it is as thick as desired.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||02-21-2001||11:41 AM||||He also didn't take all the expanding foam out of the core, which makes it stronger.  I didn't use foam on mine, but I wish now that I did.  I might get some and spray it in there later.  The foam also would give it a better sound.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000003||stimpy||02-21-2001||12:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fropuff:<BR><B>He also didn't take all the expanding foam out of the core, which makes it stronger.  I didn't use foam on mine, but I wish now that I did.  I might get some and spray it in there later.  The foam also would give it a better sound.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If "he" means me, then actually I did take all the foam out once I was done with the shell. Sure, there are bits remaining in there, but nothing structural.<BR>fropuff, how did you go about making the form? I am curious about other methods. I was making it up as I went along, so I'm sure there are other good ideas out there.<BR>And g, yeah, just keep layeringmore cloth on. 1/8" will be more then thick enough.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Standard||02-22-2001||12:36 AM||||So just exactly how much fiberglass cloth did you use?  It looks like the 8 sq. feet I got will barely cover the box with one layer...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Galen||02-22-2001||01:36 AM||||Too much work for me hehe  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/galen-sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||02-22-2001||08:51 AM||||8 square feet barely enough?! My God man, how big are you making it? Have you actually started applying the glass? The stuff does spread out. Out of the 1' by 8' cloth that came in the kit, I used less then half of the cloth.  Most of the thickness comes from the resin. The glass is just there to give structural integrity to the resin, like rebar in concrete.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||02-22-2001||09:27 AM||||Unless I have the special "large-footwell" edition Fiero, it should be the same size as yours  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I guess I'm not spreading the cloth out enough...  I'll try a little more when I get some rubber gloves...<P>As for other ways of building the box, my dad gave me a great idea.  You know those blocks of blue construction foam you can buy at Menards?  Buy a few of those and glue them together with spray glue, then you can carve them into the shape of your footwell.  You can shape it using a rasp, sandpaper, etc.  When you have that done, glass over it, and when the fiberglass is cured scoop out a hole in the foam and you can use gasoline to dissolve the rest of it.  Sounds like it should work pretty good.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Alex4mula||02-22-2001||11:12 AM||||What Standard described is what I'm planning to do to shape the box. Already have the blocks glued. In my case I'll do one for the stock sub location. I'm not sure about the gas thou. Maybe dangerous. Hope to have it ready for Daytona  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||stimpy||02-22-2001||11:50 AM||||That's a great idea! I'm not really sure what kind of foam you are talking about, is it kinda like that green floral foam from craft stores? Whatever works, ya know. I'd hesitate using the gas to melt the stuff though. Sounds messy and toxic. Plus, it's OK to have some foam left attached to the glass. Kinda like the texture of am coustic ceiling.  ||||reg||14
Z||000010||Standard||02-22-2001||05:38 PM||||That was all based on hearsay from my dad  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  As far as the gas thing goes, it should evaporate and not be too much of a problem, but i was a little wary of it too.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Sage||02-22-2001||10:49 PM||||If you're using spray foam, you have to use a minimul expanding polyurethane foam, or the resin will eat it.  If you are not that concerned with getting an EXACT shape, then it doesn't matter that much.  When it melts, it doesn't completly disapear, it just kind of "sinks" to smaller than you wanted it to be.  I think the green board foam you are talking about is one that the styrene in the resin attacks and melts.  <P>As far as forms go, you can use just about anything you want that will take the shape you want to capture, and not melt when exposed to resin.  Plaster, bondo, cardboard, wood, metal, etc.  All you are using it for is to lay the glass up on till it kicks and takes on the shape and form you want to end up with.  The closer your plug(form) is to the desired end result, the less work you have to do to get the glass where you want it.  That's why GOOD glass parts are usually high cost, it takes mucho hours to create the mold, if it's done right.<P>Just some thoughts on the matter!<P>Have a good one!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000012||stimpy||02-23-2001||08:18 AM||||Thanks for the input Sage. It's good to hear from someone who knows what they are talking about. <BR>That being said, I hope folks don't get scared off the project. While making something that will look smooth and good would be very labor intensive and time consuming, this sub enclosure is not. My enclosure is hideously ugly from te backside, but no one ever sees that. The only concern was does it fit, and does it hold air.<P>As far as the expanding foam, in the method I described, the expanding foam is enveloped in heavy construction paper. While I did use the minimal expanding variety, I think the high expanding would work and make for easier clean-up.<p>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 02-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Standard||02-23-2001||12:35 PM||||Yep, there ain't no way this thing is ever gonna be pretty by any means...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  as long as it gets the job done..||||reg||1
Z||000014||fropuff||02-23-2001||02:22 PM||||Sorry that I didn't check back; and sorry for the misconception about the foam.  Anyway, I used posterboard, tagboard, whatever; all the same.  I did like stimpy did, pushed it all in the footwell, and duct taped all the little "wrinkles".  it make it very firm, enough to put the cloth on.  I only put one layer on so far, but I'm going to put another on.  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Alex4mula||02-23-2001||03:22 PM||||You can cover the floral foam with aluminum paper and duct tape. That way the resin won't get to the foam.||||reg||1
